1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,151
One part is noise. Many parts are

2
00:00:03,251 --> 00:00:07,388
information. Six faces, all equally likely.

3
00:00:07,483 --> 00:00:10,311
One roll. It tells you nothing.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,180
Ten rolls. Still lumpy.

5
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:15,518
A hundred. Evening out.

6
00:00:15,616 --> 00:00:18,204
A thousand. Nearly level.

7
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:22,208
Ten thousand: flat, one sixth each.

8
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,552
Predictable in bulk. Still flat, still one die at a time.

9
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:31,245
Now average the dice instead of counting

10
00:00:31,345 --> 00:00:33,642
them. One die is the baseline.

11
00:00:33,733 --> 00:00:37,113
Average two, and the flat top is gone.

12
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,868
Average five: a shape where there was none.

13
00:00:40,966 --> 00:00:43,554
Thirty dice, and it is a bell.

14
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:47,083
Those spreads are measured, not drawn: one die's

15
00:00:47,183 --> 00:00:50,102
spread over the square root of the count.

16
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:55,832
Subgroup size across the bottom, spread of the mean going up.

17
00:00:55,916 --> 00:00:59,656
Sigma of x bar equals sigma over root n.

18
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:05,650
Four parts halve it. Nine, a third. Sixteen, a quarter.

19
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:09,569
This is why charts watch subgroup means: a shift

20
00:01:09,669 --> 00:01:13,570
moves a mean visibly, and drowns in single parts.

